## 1. Introduction
Electrocatalysis plays a key role in energy storage/conversion, rechargeable metal-air batteries, water splitting, fuel cells and so on [1–6]. In particular, electrocatalytic water splitting has caused extensive concern because it can transform electric energy into clean energy [7,8]. To enhance the water splitting efficiency, researchers usually use noble-metal electrocatalysts (Pt-based and Ir/Ru-based) in electrolysis [9–13]. Nevertheless, the insufficient storage and high costs of these noble metals astrict their extensive application. In consequence, designing earth-abundant, low-cost and high-activity non-noble metal electrocatalysts is highly desired [14,15].
For achievement of the above objectives, researchers developed all kinds of non-noble metal nanomaterials with different morphologies and structures for water splitting [16–23]. Among them, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been considered as desired precursors for the preparation of functional transition metal-carbon-based nanohybrids, which will potentially become cheap and high-efficiency electrocatalysts for water splitting [24– 32]. The nanostructures of MOF precursors can affect the chemical and physical properties of transition metal-carbon-based nanohybrids. So construction of MOF precursors with good morphology and constituent is generally believed as a precondition to obtain these nanohybrids with excellent electrocatalytic performance [33–35]. As a result, the structural design of MOF precursors has attracted wide attention.
It is well-known that zeolite imidazole frameworks (ZIFs) are important subfamily of MOFs. ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 are typical representatives of ZIF structures that they possess zeolite-like 3D topological structures and abundant nitrogen and carbon ligands [24]. They are expected to be good candidates as the precursor templates to synthesize all kinds of metal-carbon nanohybrids. However, the inherently poor electrical conductively and stability of ZIFs extremely restrict them to take the place of noble metal electrocatalysts. One effective method for amelioration is to combine ZIFs with highly conductive substrates, for instance conductive metal, carbon nanotubes, and so on [24,36]. For example, Li et al. reported a porous carbon nanomaterial loaded with cobalt nanoparticles derived from pyrolysis of a $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{Z n}$ bimetallic ZIF, which shows prominent electrocatalytic performance [37]. While combining ZIFs with conductive material might enhance electrocatalytic performance, their catalytic activity is less studied so far. Enlightened by the prominent conductivity of Ag NWs and rich active sites of NCF, the nanohybrids that consisted of Ag NWs and NCF embedded with metal nanoparticles are expected to be effective electrocatalysts for overall water splitting [36,38]. Herein, we intended to synthesize ZnCo nanoparticles embedded into $N-$ doped carbon frameworks that grow on the surface of highly conductive Ag NWs (denoted as Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids). Owing to their large specific surface area, accessible active sites and synergistic effect among components, Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids displayed excellent electrocatalytic performance for both HER and OER on overall water splitting in alkaline solution.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Silver nitrate $(\mathsf{A g N O}_{3}$ , AR), ferric chloride $(\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}$ , CP), ethylene glycol (EG, AR, $99.5\%$ ), ethanol (AR, $99.7\%$ ), methanol (GR, $99.7\%$ , isopropanol (AR, $99.7\%$ ), zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ AR, $99\%$ ), cobaltous nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $98.5\%$ , and 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm, $99\%$ ) were all purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent CO., Ltd. Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $\mathsf{M w}=55,000$ and $\mathsf{M w}=360,000$ ) were bought from Alfa Aesar. All reagents were used as received.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ag NWs
The preparation method of Ag NWs referred to previous report [39].
## 2.3. Synthesis of AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors
Methanol was used as the solvent for synthesis of AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors [40]. Firstly, $80\mathrm{mg}$ of Ag NWs were dispersed in $500~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution. Then $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of 2-MeIm solution $(456.5\mathrm{mg})$ and $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ solution $\left(827~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were added to the above solution, respectively. The mixture was stirred at room temperature for $3\mathrm{h}$ , then standing to allow ZIF-8 growth on the surface of $\mathsf{A g}$ NWs. After growing for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors were obtained and washed with methanol for several times by centrifugation. Finally, the obtained solid products were dried at $60^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in a vacuum oven.
## 2.4. Synthesis of AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors
$100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors was dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol to form a homogeneous suspension. Then $582~\mathrm{mg}$ of $C_{0}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $616{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of 2-MeIm were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol to shape two clarified solution, respectively. Afterwards, we added $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ solution and 2-MeIm solution into the mixture solution of AgNW $@$ ZIF-8, successively. The AgNW@ZIF8@ZIF-67 precursors were obtained by stirring the reaction solution for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ The prepared sample was purified by centrifugation and dried at $60^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum oven.
## 2.5. Synthesis of Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybids
The obtained AgNW@ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ ZIF-67 precursors were put in a crucible. Then we put the crucible in a tube furnace that heated to the calcination temperature at a ramp rate of $2\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \operatorname*{min}^{-1}$ . $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F}$ nanohybrids can be obtained by keeping the AgNW $@$ ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ ZIF-67 precursors at the calcination temperature $(600^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere.
## 2.6. Materials characterization and electrochemical measurement
The materials characterization and electrochemical performance tests referred to our previous report [41].
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthetic route for Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids was exhibited in Scheme 1. We adopted a step-by-step method to prepare Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids. Firstly, Ag NWs were prepared by a solvothermal approach. As shown in Fig. 1a and b, the assynthesized 1D Ag NWs possessed uniform morphology and size. The average diameter was about $35~\mathrm{{nm}}$ which can promote ZIF-8 coating. Subsequently, Ag NWs were employed as templates to synthesize AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of AgNW@ZIF-8 was exhibited in Fig. 1c that the ZIF-8 was successfully grown on the surface of Ag NWs. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image showed that the nanoshells were rugged and the thickness of nanoshells was about $35\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1d). It can be seen that AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors exhibit lantern-like core-shell structures. Besides, ZIF-8 nanoshells have a porous structure that can absorb cobalt precursor, which is conductive to the epitaxial growth of ZIF-67 on the surface of ZIF-8.
After that, AgNW@ZIF-8 precursors were further coordinated with $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and 2-MeIM to allow for nanocrystals growth at room temperature, forming the AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors. Fig. 2a displayed the SEM image of the AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF67 precursors. We could discover that ZIF-67 grow on the surface of AgNW@ZIF-8 without separate ZIF-67 nanocrystals. TEM image of AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 was shown in Fig. 2b, the average diameter of AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 was about $115\ \mathrm{nm}$ . X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 further showed that ZIF67 was successfully grown on the surface of AgNW@ZIF-8 (Fig. S1). Since ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 crystals have parallel unit cell parameters, we speculate that the ZIF-67 is likely to formation on the surface of AgNW@ZIF-8 via epitaxial growth [24]. In addition, we tried to directly synthesize ZIF-67 on the surface of Ag NWs. As shown in Fig. S2a, ZIF-67 could not grow well on the surface of nanowires.
According to the thermogravimetric (TG) curve tested under a $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, the weight of the AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors decreased rapidly along with continual temperature rise because the organic ligands were pyrolyzed into carbon frameworks by thermal treatment under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Fig. S3). On the basis of TG curve, the AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors were heat-treated at 400, 600 or $800~^{\circ}C$ for $^\mathrm{~3~h~}$ under a $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ flow (denoted as $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-}\chi,$ , $x=400$ , 600, 800), respectively. The TEM images of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F}$ nanohybrids are exhibited in Fig. 2c and Fig. S4b–d. At the pyrolysis temperature of $400~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the $\scriptstyle\mathtt{A g N W@Z I F-8@Z I F-67}$ precursor was not completely pyrolyzed (Fig. S4b). The Ag NWs at $800~^{\circ}C$ were unstable, which affected the pyrolyzation of ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\alpha}}$ ZIF-67 precursors (Fig. S4d). In comparison, the Ag NWs were well preserved the morphology at the pyrolysis temperature of $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and ZIF-8@ZIF-67 nanoshells completely converted into $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon frameworks embedded with ZnCo nanoparticles (Fig. 2c and Fig. S4c). Besides, we synthesized ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ ZIF-67 precursors and pyrolyzed them at $600~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain ${\cal Z}\mathrm{nCo}/\mathrm{NCF}$ nanohybrids, as shown in Fig. S2b. We also calcined ${\sf A g@Z I F-8}$ precursors at $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain $\mathsf{A g@Z n/N C F}$ nanohybrids, as exhibited in Fig. S4a. The above nanohybrids will be used as control samples for subsequent electrochemical performance comparisons.
To unveil the detail microstructure, we further characterized Ag@ZnCo/NCF-600 by high resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) and the HRTEM image exhibited obvious lattice fringes (Fig. 2d). The lattice fringe spacing was about $0.206\ \mathrm{nm}$ which matched with the (111) lattice plane of metallic Zn and Co (inset in Fig. 2d). High-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image and energydispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mappings uncover that Zn, Co, C and N elements are evenly distributed on the surface of Ag NWs (Fig. 3). The elemental composition of the $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o}/$ NCF-600 is shown in Table S1 that the weight percentage of ZnCo was about $18.32\%$ . The above characterization results indicated that ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids successfully coated Ag NWs. According to the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms and pore size distribution curve, the BET surface area of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F{-}600}$ nanohybrids was about $155.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and average pore size was around $4.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S5). $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-600}$ nanohybrids possessed large specific surface area and porous structure which is helpful to enhance the electrocatalytic performance.
AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors were transformed into $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F}$ nanohybrids by pyrolysis. In the process of pyrolysis, AgNW@ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors could release $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ which were reduced to ultrafine ZnCo nanoparticles under the high temperature [42]. Meanwhile the obtained ZnCo nanoparticles could embed into N-doped porous carbon frameworks that were converted by the coordinated organic ligands. Moreover, profiting from the templates of Ag NWs, these ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids were not easy to agglomerate and very stable during electrochemical performance testing.
To ulteriorly uncover the phase transformation, we exhibited the XRD patterns of Ag@ZnCo/NCF nanohybrids at different pyrolysis temperatures (Fig. 4a). When pyrolysis temperature at $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ the XRD pattern exhibited that ZIF-8@ZIF-67 precursors incompletely convert to N-doped carbon frameworks embedded with ZnCo nanoparticles (Fig. 4a, black line). The XRD pattern of Ag@ZnCo/NCF-600 nanohybrids showed a good crystalline structure which matches with Ag (PDF No. 04-0783), Co (PDF No. 15- 0806), Zn (PDF No. 04-0831) and C (PDF No. 26-1077) (Fig. 4a, red line). With the pyrolysis temperature increasing, the XRD pattern of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-800}$ nanohybrids shows excrescent peaks owing to the unstability of Ag NWs (Fig. 4a, blue line) [43–45].
In addition, to uncover the chemical states and bonding states of elements, we carried out X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) toward $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F{-}}600$ nanohybrids. Fig. 4b was the high-resolution Ag 3d spectrum that exhibited representative peaks of Ag metal at 368.2 $(3\mathsf{d}_{5/2})$ and 374.2 eV $(3\mathsf{d}_{3/2})$ . The high-resolution $Z\mathtt{n}2\mathtt{p}$ spectrum in Fig. 4c uncovered typical peaks of Zn metal at 1021.7 $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ and 1044 eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ [46]. As shown in Fig. 4d, high-resolution Co $\mathsf{2p}$ spectrum demonstrated two prominent bands at 778.8 and $793.79\ \mathrm{eV}$ which can be assigned to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ . The existence of satellite peaks at 782 and $780.5\ \mathrm{~eV}$ were ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathsf{C o C}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{N}_{\mathrm{z}}$ peaks that indicated the chemical coupling among Co, C and N species. The existence of satellite peaks at 786.1 and $801.09\ \mathrm{eV}$ is due to the unavoidable oxidation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ to CoO by air in the storage and testing processes [42,43,47]. The N 1s (Fig. 4e) and C 1s (Fig. 4f) spectra could further confirm the chemical coupling among Zn, Co, N and C elements [32,33]. These results suggested that AgNW@ZIF- $\cdot8@$ ZIF-67 precursors were successfully converted into $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o}/$ NCF nanohybrids.
Composition and structure of nanomaterials play decisive role on electrocatalytic performance. Inspired by the 1D structure and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon frameworks, we assessed the electrocatalytic properties of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F}$ nanohybrids toward HER by employing a representative three-electrode method in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH. The reaction of HER in alkaline conditions is as follow [7]:
$$
4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\ +\ 4\mathrm{e}^{-}\ \rightarrow\ 2\mathrm{H}_{2}\ +\ 4\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
To exhibit the ascendant HER performace of Ag@ZnCo/NCF600, we also measured the electrochemical performance of $\mathsf{A g@Z n/N C F-600}$ , $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-400}$ , Ag@ZnCo/NCF-800 and ZnCo/NCF-600. The above samples were evenly loaded onto commercial carbon black and then dispersed in isopropanol solution. Subsequently, by adding Nafion, the solutions became more cohesive. We dropped the above solutions onto different glassy carbon electrodes to prepare working electrodes, respectively. The linear scan voltammetry (LSV) curves of diverse samples are demonstrated in Fig. 5a and Fig. S6a. The overpotential of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o}_{j}$ /NCF-600 is $139~\mathrm{\mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , which is lower than $\mathsf{A g@Z n/N C F-600}$ $\cdot417\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mV}},$ , $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F–400}$ ( $208~\mathrm{\mV})$ , $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-800}$ $\langle303\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{mV}\rangle$ (Fig. 5b), ZnCo/NCF-600 $(215~\mathrm{mV})$ and those of most previously reported electrocatalysts (Table S2). These results demonstrated that $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-600}$ nanohybrids possess better electrocatalytic performance because of their excellent structural stability and abundant active sites.
Furthermore, the Tafel curves obtained by linear fitting of polarization curves were used to assess the catalytic kinetics of the samples. Evidently, the Tafel slope of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F{-}600}$ is $108~\mathrm{{mV}}$ dec1 which lower than these of $\mathsf{A g@Z n/N C F-600}$ $(125~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), Ag@ZnCo/NCF-400 $(178\quad\mathrm{mV}\quad\mathrm{dec}^{-1}.$ , and $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F{-}800}$ $(153~\mathrm{~mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}).$ ) (Fig. 5c). Therefore, the lower Tafel slope of Ag@ZnCo/NCF-600 has markedly favorable HER kinetics in alkaline solutions [48,49]. Afterwards, we performed cyclic stability tests on $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-600}$ nanohybrids in alkaline media. The LSV curves of $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F{-}600}$ demonstrated a negligible loss in current density compared to initial curve before and after 1000 cyclic voltammetry cycles (Fig. 5d). Those results demonstrated that $\mathsf{A g@Z n C o/N C F-600}$ nanohybrids have ascendant electrocatalytic performance toward HER in alkaline media.
